WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
In Australia, E10 petrol is up to ten

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.400
percent ethanol.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.440
Litre for litre, ethanol carries less energy than

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:08.320
petrol.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.320
So why blend it in at all?

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.880
Burning an alcohol tells you two things that

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.440
matter for choosing a fuel: how much energy

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.560
you get, and where the carbon came from.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.880
So what does burning an alcohol tell us

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.080
about fuel choice?

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.620
Start with the equation.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.140
With plenty of oxygen, ethanol burns completely to

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.020
carbon dioxide and water.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.140
Balance carbon first: two carbons, so two C

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:35.700
O two.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.940
Then hydrogen: six hydrogens, so three waters.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.620
Now oxygen: four on the right, plus three,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.260
is seven.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.140
But ethanol brings one oxygen of its own,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.020
so it needs six more: three O two.

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:54.433
Methanol works the same way, but it needs

00:00:54.594 --> 00:00:56.113
one and a half O two.

00:00:56.354 --> 00:00:59.233
That's fine: a combustion equation is usually written

00:00:59.314 --> 00:01:01.633
for one mole of fuel, so the enthalpy

00:01:01.714 --> 00:01:03.553
change belongs to that mole.

00:01:03.874 --> 00:01:06.593
For methanol, delta H is about minus seven

00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:09.273
hundred and twenty six kilojoules per mole.

00:01:09.554 --> 00:01:12.353
It's negative because burning releases heat, as you

00:01:12.434 --> 00:01:13.633
saw in Module 5.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:19.393
If oxygen runs short, combustion is incomplete.

00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:22.353
Some carbon ends up as carbon monoxide, a

00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:25.473
toxic gas, or as soot, which is solid

00:01:25.554 --> 00:01:26.033
carbon.

00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:28.753
You can see it: the flame turns yellow

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:31.633
and smoky, and black soot coats whatever is

00:01:31.754 --> 00:01:32.193
above it.

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:36.193
Less carbon reaches carbon dioxide, so less energy

00:01:36.274 --> 00:01:36.993
is released.

00:01:37.474 --> 00:01:40.193
So soot tells you about the oxygen supply,

00:01:40.314 --> 00:01:42.193
not just about which fuel burned.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.800
How do we know how much energy a

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:46.880
fuel gives?

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.000
Burn a weighed amount under a can of

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:49.720
water.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.920
Here, zero point seven four grams of methanol

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.320
warmed a hundred grams of water by twenty

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.160
degrees.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.720
From the neutralisation video, q equals m c

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.040
delta T, with m the mass of the

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.000
water: eight point three six kilojoules.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.440
That's about eleven kilojoules per gram.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.280
Careful measurements give about twenty three, so this

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.520
can caught only about half the energy.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.320
The practical video shows where the rest goes.

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:18.853
Now the data values.

00:02:19.094 --> 00:02:22.253
Methanol, minus seven hundred and twenty six.

00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:27.173
Then ethanol, propan-one-ol and butan-one-ol, each about six

00:02:27.254 --> 00:02:29.813
hundred and fifty kilojoules per mole more.

00:02:30.134 --> 00:02:31.173
Why so steady?

00:02:31.654 --> 00:02:34.133
Each step adds one C H two group.

00:02:34.614 --> 00:02:37.413
Burning it means breaking two C H bonds,

00:02:37.454 --> 00:02:40.133
a C C bond and some oxygen bonds,

00:02:40.174 --> 00:02:42.773
but it forms one more carbon dioxide and

00:02:42.854 --> 00:02:44.293
one more water.

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:46.933
Forming those bonds releases more energy than the

00:02:47.134 --> 00:02:48.293
breaking takes.

00:02:48.414 --> 00:02:51.333
Same extra group, about the same extra energy.

00:02:53.334 --> 00:02:55.973
But a bigger molecule also weighs more.

00:02:56.214 --> 00:02:58.773
For a fuel you carry around, energy per

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:01.173
gram matters more than per mole.

00:03:01.734 --> 00:03:05.013
Divide by molar mass: ethanol gives about thirty

00:03:05.094 --> 00:03:06.373
kilojoules per gram.

00:03:06.774 --> 00:03:09.893
Octane, a petrol molecule, gives about forty eight.

00:03:10.454 --> 00:03:11.813
Why less for ethanol?

00:03:12.294 --> 00:03:15.493
Its oxygen adds mass but no energy, and

00:03:15.574 --> 00:03:18.373
one carbon is already bonded to oxygen, so

00:03:18.454 --> 00:03:20.693
it's partly oxidised before it starts.

00:03:21.174 --> 00:03:24.453
Per litre, ethanol carries about thirty percent less

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:25.733
energy than petrol.

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:29.733
So why use ethanol at all?

00:03:30.294 --> 00:03:33.013
Burn ethanol or petrol completely, and you get

00:03:33.094 --> 00:03:36.213
the same products: carbon dioxide and water.

00:03:36.774 --> 00:03:39.253
The difference is where the carbon came from.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:42.133
Ethanol can be fermented from sugar in crops

00:03:42.214 --> 00:03:45.253
like sugarcane, and the cane took carbon dioxide

00:03:45.334 --> 00:03:46.693
from the air as it grew.

00:03:47.254 --> 00:03:49.893
So burning that ethanol returns carbon that was

00:03:50.094 --> 00:03:51.373
recently in the air.

00:03:51.654 --> 00:03:54.453
Petrol's carbon was locked underground for millions of

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:57.893
years, so burning it adds extra carbon dioxide

00:03:57.934 --> 00:03:58.613
to the air.

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:03.433
That doesn't make bioethanol carbon neutral, though.

00:04:03.914 --> 00:04:07.353
As you saw last video, distilling takes energy,

00:04:07.434 --> 00:04:10.793
and so do growing, harvesting and fermenting, often

00:04:10.874 --> 00:04:12.233
from fossil fuels.

00:04:12.554 --> 00:04:14.873
The crop also needs land and water that

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:15.993
could grow food.

00:04:16.634 --> 00:04:19.113
So ethanol's case is a renewable source that

00:04:19.194 --> 00:04:22.393
can add less net carbon dioxide, against less

00:04:22.474 --> 00:04:25.033
energy per litre and the resources to grow

00:04:25.114 --> 00:04:25.433
it.

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:30.553
Petrol has costs before it's ever burned, because

00:04:30.594 --> 00:04:32.713
the hydrocarbons are taken from the Earth.

00:04:33.674 --> 00:04:38.153
Drilling, mining and fracking bring economic benefits: jobs,

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:41.193
export income and a secure energy supply.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.793
But they carry environmental risks, like oil spills,

00:04:44.874 --> 00:04:47.913
habitat loss and possible water contamination.

00:04:48.234 --> 00:04:51.913
And there are sociocultural costs, affecting communities and

00:04:51.994 --> 00:04:52.473
culture.

00:04:53.114 --> 00:04:55.513
They often fall on communities near the site,

00:04:55.754 --> 00:04:58.553
including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:01.233
their Country, rather than on those burning the

00:05:01.274 --> 00:05:01.833
fuel.

00:05:03.834 --> 00:05:05.113
So which fuel?

00:05:05.594 --> 00:05:07.913
Judge each on its energy per gram and

00:05:07.994 --> 00:05:11.033
per litre, where its carbon comes from, what

00:05:11.194 --> 00:05:13.913
producing it costs, and what obtaining it does

00:05:13.954 --> 00:05:15.673
to land and people.

00:05:15.794 --> 00:05:17.513
Petrol wins on energy.

00:05:17.594 --> 00:05:20.153
Ethanol wins on a renewable source.

00:05:20.234 --> 00:05:22.553
That's one reason blends like E10 are a

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:24.873
compromise, rather than a clear winner.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.020
Two lines to keep.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.220
Per mole, each C H two adds about

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:32.580
six fifty.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.500
Per gram, oxygen costs energy.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.340
And whichever alcohol you burn, you get carbon

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.700
dioxide and water, unless the oxygen runs short.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:44.260
Your turn.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.940
Butan-one-ol is burned in a spirit burner beneath

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.300
a metal can of water.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:53.140
Write a balanced equation for its complete combustion.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.100
After a few minutes, a black deposit forms

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.300
on the base of the can.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.220
Explain how this deposit forms.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.940
Pause here to answer.

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:15.753
C four H nine O H, plus six

00:06:15.807 --> 00:06:18.686
O two, gives four C O two plus

00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:20.526
five H two O.

00:06:20.607 --> 00:06:23.806
Thirteen oxygens on the right, and butan-one-ol brings

00:06:23.967 --> 00:06:26.686
one, so twelve from O two.

00:06:26.767 --> 00:06:28.446
Six and a half O two forgets the

00:06:28.527 --> 00:06:30.046
oxygen in the O H.

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:33.486
The black deposit is soot: carbon from incomplete

00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:36.526
combustion, because the flame got too little oxygen

00:06:36.607 --> 00:06:38.126
for the amount of fuel burning.

00:06:40.134 --> 00:06:40.693
Last one.

00:06:40.774 --> 00:06:44.693
Ethanol's molar enthalpy of combustion is minus thirteen

00:06:44.854 --> 00:06:48.373
sixty seven kilojoules per mole, and octane's is

00:06:48.454 --> 00:06:50.373
minus fifty four seventy one.

00:06:51.014 --> 00:06:52.693
Their molar masses are on screen.

00:06:53.414 --> 00:06:56.133
Calculate the energy released per gram of each

00:06:56.214 --> 00:06:56.693
fuel.

00:06:57.094 --> 00:07:00.213
Then compare them as fuels, including one advantage

00:07:00.254 --> 00:07:02.453
of ethanol that isn't about energy.

00:07:03.014 --> 00:07:04.213
Pause here to answer.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:17.593
Divide each by its molar mass.

00:07:18.074 --> 00:07:21.353
Ethanol: twenty nine point six seven kilojoules per

00:07:21.434 --> 00:07:22.073
gram.

00:07:22.314 --> 00:07:25.513
Octane: forty seven point nine one.

00:07:25.634 --> 00:07:29.193
So octane releases more energy per gram.

00:07:29.314 --> 00:07:32.473
Per mole, octane looks four times better, but

00:07:32.554 --> 00:07:36.033
its molecules are heavier, so compare per gram.

00:07:36.314 --> 00:07:39.513
Ethanol's advantage: it can be made from crops,

00:07:39.594 --> 00:07:42.153
a renewable source that took carbon dioxide from

00:07:42.234 --> 00:07:43.673
the air as it grew.

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:49.060
So an alcohol's energy grows with its chain,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:52.020
its oxygen lowers the energy per gram, and

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.900
choosing a fuel weighs energy against carbon and

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.020
cost.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:59.940
Next: measuring these enthalpies yourself, and explaining why

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:01.700
your answer comes out low.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:05.140
Here are the key notes.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.300
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.180
to copy them.
